# Searching Algorithms: Finding Needles in Data Haystacks

> Sorting (the previous lesson) exists largely so that searching can
> be fast. This lesson contrasts the two fundamental search
> strategies — scan-everything linear search versus halve-and-repeat
> binary search — then shows how hash tables make lookup
> effectively instant, and why all of it matters at real-world
> scale.

## 1. The Problem: Membership and Lookup

Two everyday questions reduce to search:

- **Membership:** is this word in the dictionary?
- **Retrieval:** what value does this key map to (a phone number for
  a name)?

[definition]
A searching algorithm takes a collection and a target key, and
returns the key's location (or its associated value), or reports
"not found." Its cost is measured in **comparisons** or **lookups**
as a function of collection size $n$.
[/definition]

## 2. Linear Search: Simple, Universal, Slow

Check items one by one until the target appears or the list ends:

```text
LINEAR-SEARCH(A, target):
  for i in 0 .. length(A) - 1:
    if A[i] == target:
      return i
  return NOT-FOUND
```

| Case | Comparisons | Time |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Best (first item) | 1 | O(1) |
| Average | n/2 | O(n) |
| Worst (absent / last) | n | O(n) |

Linear search needs **no ordering** and works on linked lists,
streams, anything iterable — often the right choice for small or
unsorted data.

## 3. Binary Search: Halve the World

**Requirement:** the array must be **sorted**. Compare the target
with the middle element; discard the impossible half; repeat:

```text
BINARY-SEARCH(A, target):
  low = 0; high = length(A) - 1
  while low <= high:
    mid = (low + high) / 2   (floor)
    if A[mid] == target:  return mid
    if A[mid] <  target:  low  = mid + 1
    else:                 high = mid - 1
  return NOT-FOUND
```

### Worked example: search 23 in

@diagram tree id="binary-search-trace" layout="tree" aria="Binary search halving steps for target 23"
  "[2 5 8 12 16 23 38 56 72 91] mid=16" -> "[23 38 56 72 91] mid=56"
  "[23 38 56 72 91] mid=56" -> "[23 38] mid=23"
  "[23 38] mid=23" -> "FOUND at index 5"
@/diagram

| Step | Range remaining | mid | Discard |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 10 items | 16 | everything left of 16 |
| 2 | 5 items | 56 | 56 and above |
| 3 | 2 items | 23 | found ✓ |

Three comparisons instead of six; the gap only widens with size:

@chart bar title="Worst-case comparisons for n = 1,000,000"
labels:
Linear
Binary

data:
1000000
20
@/chart

[important]
Each step halves the candidates, so binary search runs in
**O(log n)**: a million items take ≤ 20 probes, a billion ≤ 30.
But the array must stay sorted — one insertion in the middle costs
O(n) shifting, which is why databases pair search with careful
insertion structures (see the sorting lesson's trade-offs).
[/important]

[tip]
Overflow bug: `mid = (low + high) / 2` can overflow in languages with
fixed-size integers; write `mid = low + (high - low) / 2`. This bug
lived in Java's own library for years — Bentley's *Programming
Pearls* famously asked professional programmers to write binary
search and most got it wrong. Test with empty arrays, one element,
and targets at both ends.
[/tip]

## 4. Beyond Comparison: Hash Tables

Sorting is not the only route to speed. A **hash table** computes a
storage slot directly from the key:

1. A **hash function** maps the key to an index — e.g.
   `h(key) = key mod 10` for an array of 10 buckets.
2. Collisions (two keys, one bucket) are resolved by **chaining**
   (a list per bucket) or **open addressing** (probe onward).

| Operation | Average | Worst (chaining, poor hash) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Insert | O(1) | O(n) |
| Lookup | O(1) | O(n) |
| Delete | O(1) | O(n) |

Hash tables trade order for speed: no sorted traversal, but
instant membership — the engine behind Python's `dict`, Java's
`HashMap`, JavaScript objects, caches, deduplication sets, database
indexes, and blockchain address lookups.

[definition]
Two ideas to keep distinct: **binary search = O(log n) lookup on a
sorted array** (no extra memory); **hash table = O(1) average
lookup with O(n) extra memory and no ordering**. Choose by whether
you need range queries (sorted wins) or pure point lookups (hash
wins).
[/definition]

## 5. Variations Worth Knowing

- **Interpolation search** — for uniformly distributed sorted keys,
  guess where the target should be (like opening a phone book near
  'S' for 'Sharma'): O(log log n) average, but degrades to O(n) on
  skewed data.
- **Exponential (galloping) search** — double the index until past
  the target, then binary-search the last interval; ideal for
  unbounded streams.
- **Depth-first / breadth-first search** — searching *graphs* and
  trees (next: the graph algorithms lesson), not arrays.
- **Binary search on the answer** — when a property is monotonic
  ("can we finish within time x?"), binary-search the value itself:
  the pattern behind "minimum maximum capacity" interview classics
  and judge time-limit tooling.

## 6. Real-World Scale

- **Git** stores every object by SHA-1 hash — object lookup is a
  hash-table probe, not a search.
- **Databases** use B-trees — multi-way search trees keeping log
  height tiny for disk-sized data (one node per page).
- **Spell-checkers** keep a hash set of dictionary words for O(1)
  "is this a word?"
- Your **browser cache**, OS **page tables**, and **DNS resolvers**
  are all, at bottom, hash tables with clever eviction.

[summary]
Linear search is O(n) and universal; binary search is O(log n) but
demands sorted data — halving the candidate set each comparison,
20 probes suffice for a million items. Hash tables skip comparisons
entirely by computing positions, giving O(1) average lookup at the
cost of memory and ordering. Variations (interpolation, galloping,
search-on-answer) tune the trade-off, and B-trees scale the idea to
disk. Choosing between them is a matter of data order, memory, and
whether you need ranges or just point lookups.
[/summary]
